************** G E N E R A L   I N F O R M A T I O N *************9228 1451  100
                                                                  9228 1451  101
This evaluated data file is based primarily on the nuclear model  9228 1451  102
code Talys [kon07], version 1.4.                                  9228 1451  103
                                                                  9228 1451  104
All transport data for neutrons, charged particles, photons and   9228 1451  105
residual nuclides are filed using a combination of MF1,2,3,4,5,6, 9228 1451  106
12,14,15. This includes cross sections, angular distributions,    9228 1451  107
double-differential spectra, discrete and continuum photon        9228 1451  108
production cross sections, and residual production (activation)   9228 1451  109
cross sections. Moreover, isomeric production data are stored in  9228 1451  110
MF8,9,10.  The data file has been created automatically using     9228 1451  111
the ENDF-6 format generator TEFAL.                                9228 1451  112
                                                                  9228 1451  113
                                                                  9228 1451  114
                                                                  9228 1451  115
*************************** T H E O R Y **************************9228 1451  116
                                                                  9228 1451  117
Talys is a computer code system for the prediction and analysis   9228 1451  118
of nuclear reactions. Talys simulates reactions that involve      9228 1451  119
neutrons, gamma-rays, protons, deuterons, tritons, helions and    9228 1451  120
alpha-particles, in the 1 keV - 200 MeV energy range and for      9228 1451  121
target nuclides of mass 12 and heavier. This is achieved by       9228 1451  122
implementing a suite of nuclear reaction models into a single     9228 1451  123
code system. It enables to evaluate nuclear reactions from        9228 1451  124
the unresolved resonance region up to intermediate energies. This 9228 1451  125
evaluation is based on a theoretical analysis that utilizes the   9228 1451  126
optical model, compound nucleus statistical theory, fission,      9228 1451  127
direct reactions and pre-equilibrium processes, in combination    9228 1451  128
with databases and models for nuclear structure. The following    9228 1451  129
output of Talys is stored in this data file:                      9228 1451  130
                                                                  9228 1451  131
- Total, elastic and non-elastic cross sections                   9228 1451  132
- Elastic scattering angular distributions                        9228 1451  133
- Inelastic cross sections to discrete states                     9228 1451  134
- Inelastic scattering angular distributions to discrete states   9228 1451  135
- Exclusive channel cross sections, e.g. (n,g), (n,2n), (n,np),.. 9228 1451  136
- Exclusive channel energy spectra                                9228 1451  137
- Exclusive channel double-differential spectra                   9228 1451  138
- Exclusive gamma production for discrete states and continuum    9228 1451  139
- Isomeric and ground state cross sections                        9228 1451  140
- Residual production cross sections                              9228 1451  141
- Recoil data                                                     9228 1451  142
- Total particle cross sections, e.g. (n,xn), (n,xp),..           9228 1451  143
- Total particle energy spectra                                   9228 1451  144
- Total particle double-differential spectra                      9228 1451  145
                                                                  9228 1451  146
Here follows a short description of the used nuclear models:      9228 1451  147
                                                                  9228 1451  148
##### OPTICAL MODEL                                               9228 1451  149
                                                                  9228 1451  150
All optical model calculations are performed by ECIS-06 [ray94],  9228 1451  151
in Talys used as a subroutine. The default optical model          9228 1451  152
potentials (OMP) used are the local and global parameterizations  9228 1451  153
of Koning and Delaroche [kon03]. These are phenomenological OMPs  9228 1451  154
for neutrons and protons which in principle are valid over the    9228 1451  155
1 keV - 200 MeV energy range, though the low energy boundary of   9228 1451  156
validity may differ from nucleus to nucleus. For neutrons and     9228 1451  157
protons, the used parameterization is given in Eq. (7) of         9228 1451  158
[kon03]. Revised versions of these potentials (unpublished) are   9228 1451  159
used in the case of deformed nuclides.                            9228 1451  160
To calculate the transmission coefficients and reaction           9228 1451  161
cross sections for deuterons, tritons, helions and alpha          9228 1451  162
particles, we use OMPs that are directly derived from our nucleon 9228 1451  163
potentials using Watanabe's folding approach.                     9228 1451  164
                                                                  9228 1451  165
##### DIRECT REACTIONS                                            9228 1451  166
                                                                  9228 1451  167
The built-in ECIS-06 is used for DWBA or coupled-channels         9228 1451  168
calculations for rotational or vibrational (or a combination of   9228 1451  169
these) nuclides, depending on the information present in the      9228 1451  170
nuclear structure database of Talys.                              9228 1451  171
In addition, a macroscopic, phenomenological model to describe    9228 1451  172
giant resonances in the inelastic channel is used. For each       9228 1451  173
multipolarity an energy weighted sum rule applies and a DWBA      9228 1451  174
calculation with ECIS-06 is performed for each giant resonance    9228 1451  175
state. The cross section is then spread over the continuum with a 9228 1451  176
Gaussian distribution.                                            9228 1451  177
                                                                  9228 1451  178
##### COMPOUND NUCLEUS                                            9228 1451  179
                                                                  9228 1451  180
For binary compound nucleus reactions we use the model of         9228 1451  181
Moldauer, i.e. the Hauser-Feshbach model corrected for width      9228 1451  182
fluctuations. The transmission coefficients have been generated   9228 1451  183
with the aforementioned OMPs and the full j,l-dependence of the   9228 1451  184
transmission coefficients in the Hauser-Feshbach model is used.   9228 1451  185
For each nucleus that can be reached through a binary reaction,   9228 1451  186
several discrete levels and a continuum described by level        9228 1451  187
densities are included simultaneously as competing channels.      9228 1451  188
                                                                  9228 1451  189
The compound nucleus angular distributions are calculated with    9228 1451  190
the Blatt-Biedenharn formalism, leading to compound nucleus       9228 1451  191
Legendre coefficients that are added to their direct              9228 1451  192
counterparts. For multiple compound emission, i.e. emitted        9228 1451  193
particles after the binary emission, we use the Hauser-Feshbach   9228 1451  194
model. Again, for each residual nucleus several discrete states   9228 1451  195
are included as well as a continuum described by level densities. 9228 1451  196
Multiple compound emission is continued until all reaction        9228 1451  197
channels are closed and the population distribution of all        9228 1451  198
residual nuclides is depleted, through gamma decay, until they    9228 1451  199
end up in the ground state or in an isomer.                       9228 1451  200
                                                                  9228 1451  201
For the level density, we take the Constant Temperature Model     9228 1451  202
with level density parameters as given in [kon08]. This employs   9228 1451  203
the temperature law at low energies and a Fermi gas expression at 9228 1451  204
high energies, and takes into account the damping of shell        9228 1451  205
effects at high excitation energy. We have obtained the level     9228 1451  206
density parameters from a simultaneous fit to all experimental    9228 1451  207
discrete levels and D0 values from the RIPL library [rip09].      9228 1451  208
                                                                  9228 1451  209
Gamma-ray transmission coefficients are generated with the        9228 1451  210
Kopecky-Uhl generalized Lorentzian for strength functions, with   9228 1451  211
giant dipole resonance parameters taken from the RIPL library     9228 1451  212
[rip09], and normalized with experimental radiative widths.       9228 1451  213
                                                                  9228 1451  214
##### PRE-EQUILIBRIUM REACTIONS                                   9228 1451  215
                                                                  9228 1451  216
For pre-equilibrium reactions, which become important for         9228 1451  217
incident energies above about 10 MeV, we use the two-component    9228 1451  218
exciton model [kon04], in which the neutron or proton types of    9228 1451  219
particles and holes are followed throughout the reaction. For     9228 1451  220
energies above 20 MeV, multiple pre-equilibrium emission up to    9228 1451  221
any order of particle emission was included in the calculations.  9228 1451  222
A parameterization for the squared matrix element is used that is 9228 1451  223
valid for the whole energy range of this evaluation.              9228 1451  224
                                                                  9228 1451  225
For deuterons, tritons, helions and alpha-particles, an extra     9228 1451  226
contribution was added from the pick/up and knock-out reaction    9228 1451  227
model by Kalbach [kal05].                                         9228 1451  228
                                                                  9228 1451  229
For photons, the model of Akkermans and Gruppelaar [akk85] was    9228 1451  230
applied, to simulate the direct and semi-direct capture           9228 1451  231
processes.                                                        9228 1451  232
                                                                  9228 1451  233
The angular distribution systematics by Kalbach [kal88] were used 9228 1451  234
to describe the angular distributions for all continuum           9228 1451  235
particles. An isotopic distribution for photons was adopted.      9228 1451  236
                                                                  9228 1451  237
                                                                  9228 1451  238
***************** F I L E   I N F O R M A T I O N ****************9228 1451  239
                                                                  9228 1451  240
                                                                  9228 1451  241
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                9228 1451  242
                                                                  9228 1451  243
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       9228 1451  244
calculated with Talys. If the maximal cross section in an         9228 1451  245
excitation function over the whole energy range does not exceed   9228 1451  246
1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not included at all. Cross sections     9228 1451  247
lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have no physical meaning and   9228 1451  248
are set to zero. We present only the most important reactions     9228 1451  249
explicitly with a separate MT-number. The minor reaction channels 9228 1451  250
are also present but they are stored in MT5 and can be            9228 1451  251
reproduced in combination with MF6. All MT numbers extend up to   9228 1451  252
the highest incident energy.                                      9228 1451  253
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:9228 1451  254
                                                                  9228 1451  255
                                                                  9228 1451  256
                                                                  9228 1451  257
                                                                  9228 1451  258
 From the energy of 1 keV up to 30 MeV, seven states [lop04]      9228 1451  259
 Coupled Channel Calculations were performed using the ECIS06     9228 1451  260
 [ray94] code which also provides compound nucleus cross sections 9228 1451  261
 and transmission coefficients used in pre-equilibrium/evaporation9228 1451  262
 emission code TALYS [kon07] (TALYS-1.4 version). This reaction   9228 1451  263
 code includes a relatively simplified fission model taking into  9228 1451  264
 account Triple Humped Fission Barrier (THFB) [lop04], explicitly 9228 1451  265
 coupling class I, II and III states.                             9228 1451  266
 Emission of light hadrons (p, He4) are explicitly treated in     9228 1451  267
 the model calculations. Fission decay of associated residual     9228 1451  268
 nuclei is also treated.                                          9228 1451  269
                                                                  9228 1451  270
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  9228 1451  271
  from  0.00225 to 30 MeV, analysis based on coupled-channel      9228 1451  272
  [lop04] calculations and the exp. data.                         9228 1451  273
                                                                  9228 1451  274
                                                                  9228 1451  275
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     9228 1451  276
   0.00225 to 30 MeV, based on coupled channel [lop04] and        9228 1451  277
       statistical model calculations.                            9228 1451  278
                                                                  9228 1451  279
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            9228 1451  280
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    9228 1451  281
                                                                  9228 1451  282
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        9228 1451  283
                                                                  9228 1451  284
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  9228 1451  285
                                                                  9228 1451  286
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           9228 1451  287
                                                                  9228 1451  288
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   9228 1451  289
MT-number. As the incident energy increases, the cross section    9228 1451  290
of MT5 increases as more reaction channels are no longer stored   9228 1451  291
under a specific MT number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be     9228 1451  292
combined with MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross         9228 1451  293
sections particle production cross sections and                   9228 1451  294
(double-)differential cross sections.                             9228 1451  295
                                                                  9228 1451  296
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 9228 1451  297
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 9228 1451  298
- MT=18    : (n,f) cross sections                                 9228 1451  299
Triple Humped Fission Barriers (THFB) [lop04], explicitly         9228 1451  300
coupling class I, II and III states were used.                    9228 1451  301
The fission cross section was evaluated as close as possible to   9228 1451  302
the IAEA fission cross section standard which was retained        9228 1451  303
as fission cross section for this evaluation.                     9228 1451  304
The differences between these 2 fission cross sections were       9228 1451  305
reported on the total elastic cross section.                      9228 1451  306
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 9228 1451  307
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 9228 1451  308
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      9228 1451  309
For the MT=81-90(n,n')cross-section of the 31th-40th excited      9228 1451  310
states. These discrete states are embedded im the contiunuum.     9228 1451  311
The corresponding cross section are originated from DWBA          9228 1451  312
calculations run with the ECIS-06 code [ray94], assuming these    9228 1451  313
direct reactions occur mainly to vibrational states.The chosen    9228 1451  314
states result from adjustment.                                    9228 1451  315
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       9228 1451  316
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              9228 1451  317
The capture cross section was evaluated as close as possible to   9228 1451  318
the ENDFB/VII.1 capture cross section which was retained          9228 1451  319
as capture cross section for this evaluation up to 170keV.        9228 1451  320
The differences between these 2 capture cross sections were       9228 1451  321
reported on the total elastic cross section.                      9228 1451  322
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  9228 1451  323
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  9228 1451  324
                                                                  9228 1451  325
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           9228 1451  326
                                                                  9228 1451  327
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    9228 1451  328
discrete levels.                                                  9228 1451  329
                                                                  9228 1451  330
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         9228 1451  331
                                                                  9228 1451  332
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  9228 1451  333
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   9228 1451  334
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      9228 1451  335
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  9228 1451  336
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      9228 1451  337
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         9228 1451  338
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   9228 1451  339
on an angular grid.                                               9228 1451  340
                                                                  9228 1451  341
- MT51-90  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-40th state       9228 1451  342
                                                                  9228 1451  343
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     9228 1451  344
                                                                  9228 1451  345
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     9228 1451  346
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   9228 1451  347
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  9228 1451  348
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             9228 1451  349
All data are generated with Talys.                                9228 1451  350
                                                                  9228 1451  351
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   9228 1451  352
                                                                  9228 1451  353
MT5 contains the production yields of particles and residual      9228 1451  354
products. It also contains the secondary energy-angle             9228 1451  355
distributions for all particles and photons which are not         9228 1451  356
represented by explicit MT numbers. First, the yields for         9228 1451  357
neutrons are given for the whole energy range. Next, on a         9228 1451  358
secondary energy grid the relative emission spectra are given     9228 1451  359
together with the parameters for the Kalbach systematics for      9228 1451  360
angular distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for    9228 1451  361
discrete states have been broadened and added to the continuum    9228 1451  362
spectra. This procedure is repeated for protons, deuterons,       9228 1451  363
tritons, Helium-3, alpha particles and photons. Finally, the      9228 1451  364
residual production yields are given per final product. All these 9228 1451  365
yields and relative distributions can be multiplied with the      9228 1451  366
cross sections given in MF3/MT5 to get the production cross       9228 1451  367
sections and (double-)differential cross sections.                9228 1451  368
                                                                  9228 1451  369
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     9228 1451  370
                                                                  9228 1451  371
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  9228 1451  372
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 9228 1451  373
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          9228 1451  374
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       9228 1451  375
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 9228 1451  376
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     9228 1451  377
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  9228 1451  378
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      9228 1451  379
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         9228 1451  380
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             9228 1451  381
                                                                  9228 1451  382
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   9228 1451  383
followed:                                                         9228 1451  384
                                                                  9228 1451  385
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9228 1451  386
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9228 1451  387
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9228 1451  388
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     9228 1451  389
                                                                  9228 1451  390
-----                                                             9228 1451  391
                                                                  9228 1451  392
##### MF=5 Neutron Energy Distributions                           9228 1451  393
 MT=18  Neutron energy distributions from fission based on the    9228 1451  394
        Los Alamos model, with multiple chances (first, second,   9228 1451  395
        third, fourth and fifth chance), and upgraded by          9228 1451  396
        G.Vladuca and A.Tudora [tud05].                           9228 1451  397
        A linear relation between the average prompt gamma ray    9228 1451  398
        energy and the average prompt neutron multiplicity and    9228 1451  399
        a dependence of the average fission fragments kinetic     9228 1451  400
        energy on the incident neutron energy are used.           9228 1451  401
        The model parameters are different from those             9228 1451  402
        adopted in [Vla01] in order to increase the low energy    9228 1451  403
        neutron emissions.                                        9228 1451  404
                                                                  9228 1451  405
 MT=455 : TAKEN FROM ENDF/B-VII.                                  9228 1451  406
                                                                  9228 1451  407
                                                                  9228 1451  408
                                                                  9228 1451  409
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 9228 1451  410
                                                                  9228 1451  411
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    9228 1451  412
for isomeric versus ground state production can be found, i.e.    9228 1451  413
for each MT number it points to either MF6, MF9 or MF10.          9228 1451  414
                                                                  9228 1451  415
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 9228 1451  416
                                                                  9228 1451  417
MF10 is used for threshold reactions.                             9228 1451  418
All data are generated with Talys. Final states with a lifetime   9228 1451  419
that exceeds 1 second are treated as isomer.                      9228 1451  420
                                                                  9228 1451  421
##### MF12: PHOTOPRODUCTION YIELD DATA                            9228 1451  422
                                                                  9228 1451  423
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:9228 1451  424
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   9228 1451  425
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   9228 1451  426
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   9228 1451  427
  given                                                           9228 1451  428
                                                                  9228 1451  429
         taken from ENDF/B-VII only for MT=18                     9228 1451  430
                                                                  9228 1451  431
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          9228 1451  432
                                                                  9228 1451  433
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  9228 1451  434
                                                                  9228 1451  435
         taken from ENDF/B-VII only for MT=18                     9228 1451  436
                                                                  9228 1451  437
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 9228 1451  438
                                                                  9228 1451  439
         taken from ENDF/B-VII for MT=18                          9228 1451  440
